2.1 Span-Exact Surgical AST Patching (TDB-702)
In early prototypes of text-based music tools, visual mutations were either unsupported or implemented by regenerating the entire text file from an abstract syntax tree—an approach that destroys user comments (%%), custom whitespace, and code organization. Conversely, naive string replacement (sourceCode.replace(oldToken, newToken)) fails as soon as a score contains two identical tokens (for example, mutating a c4:4 in Measure 12 accidentally rewrites the c4:4 in Measure 1).
In Tenuto Studio 6.0 (TDB-702), DocumentParser.attachSourceLocations() records the exact UTF-8 character span $[S_{\text{start}}, S_{\text{end}})$ and 1-based (line, col) coordinates on every compiled AtomicEvent, ClipDefinition, TrackDefinition, and FXChainItem:
$$
\text{Source}_{\text{next}} = \text{Source}[0 \dots S_{\text{start}} - 1] \;\Vert\; \text{MutatedToken} \;\Vert\; \text{Source}[S_{\text{end}} \dots N]
$$

When a user drags the right edge of a note in Measure 24 (c4:4.stacc.vol(95)~), TopologicalMutator.decomposeToken() isolates the core pitch (c4), the rational duration (:4), the chained modifier vector (['stacc', 'vol(95)']), and the tie flag (~). It computes the new rational duration in O(1) time via Euclid's algorithm (), recomposes c4:3/8.stacc.vol(95)~, and splices only bytes $[S_{\text{start}}, S_{\text{end}})$.
2.2 Continuous Knob Sweeps & mergeKey Undo Coalescing
When a producer sweeps an EQ Eight frequency knob or a channel volume fader across 60 frames, Tenuto Studio 6.0 updates the WebAudio AudioParam immediately on every pointer-move frame (zero audio latency) and dispatches ASTCommand instances tagged with a deterministic mergeKey (such as "fx:lead:eq_eight:freq1" or "mixer:vox:vol"). CommandManager.executeCommand() coalesces consecutive commands sharing the same mergeKey so that pressing Cmd + Z undoes the entire human gesture in a single step rather than stepping backward through 60 intermediate fader ticks.

3. Dual-Horizon Workspace: Unifying Non-Linear Session View with Rational Arrangement View
Ableton Live's signature workflow innovation is its dual-view paradigm: Session View for non-linear improvisation, clip launching, and scene arrangement, paired with Arrangement View for linear timeline editing, automation, and structure. Tenuto Studio 6.0 (TDB-706 through TDB-710) unifies both views inside a single .ten document.
3.1 Session View (scene and clip Blocks + Follow Actions)
In Tenuto 6.0 (TDB-701, TDB-707, TDB-708), producers can define standalone reusable clips (clip <id>) or full horizontal Session View rows (scene <index> "<Name>"):
```tenuto
clip bass_acid "303 Acid Line" track=bass scene=1 bars=2 loop=true follow=@{bars: 4, actionA: "next", actionB: "any", chanceA: 80, chanceB: 20} {
  d2:16.stacc d3:16.glide(45ms) f2:16.chance(85%) a2:16 g#2:8.marc g2:16 f2:16 |
  d2:16.stacc c2:16 d2:8 eb2:8.glide(60ms) d2:4.vel_range(90, 124) |
}
```

Quantized Launch Clock: When a user clicks a clip slot or a Scene Launch button in SessionView.tsx, the launch is scheduled onto the next global quantization boundary (meta @{ quantize_launch: "1/1" } for 1-bar launch sync, or "1/4", "1/8", "none").
Stochastic Follow Action State Machine: At the end of a clip's follow.bars duration, Tenuto's scheduler evaluates a weighted Bernoulli trial using chanceA and chanceB:  P(\text{Action}_A) = \frac{\text{chance}_A}{\text{chance}_A + \text{chance}_B}, \quad P(\text{Action}_B) = 1 - P(\text{Action}_A)  executing "next", "prev", "first", "last", "any", "other", "play_again", or "stop" automatically.
Session-to-Arrangement Recording: Clicking the Record to Arrangement button in Transport.tsx while improvising clip launches in Session View records every launched clip directly into sequential measure N { ... } blocks in the .ten source file.
3.2 Arrangement View (measure Blocks, locator Markers & Bezier Automation)
Pressing Tab switches the right viewport to ArrangementView.tsx (TDB-709, TDB-714), displaying:
Stacked Multi-Track Lanes & Group Folders: Collapsible horizontal tracks for all def, group, and return channels.
Arrangement Locators & Loop Brace: Jump markers declared via locator "Main Drop" at measure 17 render as interactive flags along the top timeline ruler.
Decoupled Z-Axis Bezier Automation Lanes (TDB-704, TDB-714): Clicking the automation toggle on any track unfolds continuous automation curves compiled from .cc(controller, [v0, v1, ...], "exp" | "linear" | "scurve") spacers, allowing producers to shape filter sweeps, pan trajectories, send levels, and tempo ramps visually or in code.

